066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124607 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124607 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124607 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124611 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124733 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124733 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124733 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124733 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124733 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124733 #edb/ref 1045 "The Multivariable Mean-Value Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124733 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124733 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124733 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124733 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4c07cab7-a385-5dcf-8fac-281a4be4946f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124760 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124760 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124760 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124760 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124760 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-y$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,2⟩,$ respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124760 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24eb3f60-8596-5c16-bdd4-fd742e474035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124760 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ (2(2)^{3}-2)-(2(0)^{3}-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨6{x}^{2},-1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =12{c}_{1}^{2}-1 \\\\ 16 & =12{c}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<2,$ it's clear that $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to the $x$-coordinate of a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}$ to find ${c}_{2}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we obtain that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125550 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125550 #edb/ref 1061 31980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125550 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "75d67410-614f-5676-9156-2ea05fc7c4ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125550 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125551 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125551 #edb/ref 1061 31981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125551 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "817eece4-7f2b-59e4-8c4a-27a84ac89bb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125551 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125552 #edb/ref 1061 31982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125552 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c6fc534-5695-5be4-8fd3-cc2b4b04687d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125552 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125557 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125557 #edb/ref 1061 32303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125557 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "00db5d1b-5ee1-52e1-8187-7aa61cad3614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125557 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125844 #edb/ref 1064 "We can extend the mean value theorem from single-variable calculus to functions of multiple variables.\n\nFirst, let's recall the mean value theorem from single-variable calculus.\n\n*If $f(x)$ is a function that's continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, then there exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\nf'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the above equation as follows:\n\n$$\nf(b) - f(a) = f'(c) (b-a)\n$$\n\nNow, we're ready to present the **multivariable mean value theorem,** which states the following: \n\n*If $f(x,y)$ is a function that's differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{c}$ such that \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{b}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}),\n$$\n\n where $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively.*\n\nThe theorem naturally extends to functions of three variables.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\n \nand the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 1,-1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle 2,2 \\rangle.$ A sketch of this situation is given below.\n\n![](images/67/67c44030beb47bf9cc53a3550da39d3a467fe45445c722803a3a8eaf70f1cb8a.png)\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ we can apply the mean value theorem. That is, there exists a point $C\\in\\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{c}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ f(2,2)-f(1,-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,2⟩-⟨1,-1⟩) \\\\ ({2}^{2}+{2}^{2})-({1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ 6 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting out the components of $\\nabla f (\\mathbf c)$ and expanding the dot product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ & =6 \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ & =6 \\\\ {f}_{x}(c)+3{f}_{y}(c) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the above equation *must* be satisfied by some point $\\mathbf c$ that lies on $\\overline{AB}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125844 #edb/ref 1065 5095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125844 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "92fbd1f5-a176-558c-9d91-081244c080f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125844 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362596 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362596 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362596 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186362597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362596 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b819c7c7-4de5-5d16-a83f-aab407f3ff76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362597 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362597 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362597 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "30cbc511-46f2-57a7-a1e6-5b9df41e93f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362598 #edb/ref 1080 "(1,\\frac{3}{2})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362598 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362598 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "67e259c2-66b9-50e9-b578-1c42714caf20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362599 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362599 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362599 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c6bb899f-cddb-5bbd-8b15-deb369bf541e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362600 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{4})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362600 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362600 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e33d1235-04f0-5510-b143-ff55309b0743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362601 #edb/ref 1080 "(1,\\sqrt{2}+1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362601 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362601 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "32a85622-d88c-5334-b282-e0da8268939c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362602 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362602 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362602 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362602 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0,-1⟩,$ respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362602 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cccbf905-9f63-54b3-9f8d-d6f0dbce85ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362602 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,-1)-f(0,2,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,0,-1⟩-⟨0,2,1⟩) \\\\ 2-(-7) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,-4y,2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},-4{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =⟨2{c}_{1},-4{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-4{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{(-2)}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{y-2}{2}=-\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,\\,y=2-2t,\\,z=1-2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2-2t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-4{c}_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t)+8(2-2t)-4(1-2t) & =9 \\\\ 2t+16-16t-4+8t & =9 \\\\ -6t & =-3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2-2(\\frac{1}{2})=1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-2(\\frac{1}{2})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{1}{2},1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362603 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362603 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362603 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186362604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362603 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ec63c256-7f17-50ef-ab3a-8b079a43b2f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362604 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{1}{2},1,0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362604 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362604 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "827f14ed-373b-54f0-87eb-ba05ac897597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362605 #edb/ref 1080 "(0,2,1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362605 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362605 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "344eb24e-1156-549a-aa63-f7b70c503bea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362606 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{5}{6},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362606 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362606 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9fa8cd0a-d89b-5bf5-ac6c-3d535195e448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362607 #edb/ref 1080 "(1,0,-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362607 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362607 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "be65387c-8a7a-5830-b531-fb3b8cd8edc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362608 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{5})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362608 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362608 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "532d3b94-1153-561c-80f6-cf5aaf59db52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399517 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399517 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399517 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399517 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,0⟩,$ respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a0cb1f4-667e-57bd-8d19-2b32f34f6a8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399517 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,0⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1,0)-f(-1,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨0,1,0⟩-⟨-1,0,1⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,0,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},0,-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨2{c}_{1},0,-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-(-1)}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ x+1 & =y=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t-1\\,\\,y=t,\\,z=1-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t-1\\,\\,{c}_{2}=t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t-1)+3(1-t)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (t-1)[2+3(t-1)] & =0 \\\\ (t-1)(3t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}-1=-\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =1-1=0, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(0,1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399518 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399518 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399518 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186399521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399518 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b7e2b45d-5b9d-5091-9941-06bd82a064b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399519 #edb/ref 1080 "$(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399519 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399519 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b63ab9ff-56cc-569e-a2eb-37c7b01b50d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399520 #edb/ref 1080 "$(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(0,1,0)$" #edb/ref 1759